hey  were  and it's starting to look like there's  actually a bunch of errors that were  happening and so I started to suspect  that this might be the problem  and in this case the thing that helped  me decide whether my hypothesis was  correct or not was getting sufficient  context here what I did is I enabled a  bunch of context about what that  histogram looked like at various times  in the past looking at it from an hour  ago a day ago in a week ago and in this  case what I could see is that these  errors were actually normal  they've been occurring for a while and  this sort of thing is really useful  because if you have a system with even  sort of a little bit of complexity there  are typically kind of infinite many  behaviors that look weird there's a  bunch of weird stuff going on at any  given time and you can't fix all of the  things that look weird when you're  trying to debug a problem so it's really  important to rule out the things that  look weird but actually are normal and  are not the cause of the thing that  you're looking at so here the historical  context helped me realize the slowness  was definitely not due to errors because  these errors are old so the next thing I  started asking is okay I have this  example of something that took 17  seconds can I look at that one example  and understand what about it took so  long so let me take a brief dry version  to talk about with distributed tracing  is Aleta gave a great talk about this  earlier today that you can watch later  if you're interested in a more detailed  explanation but in brief what  distributed tracing is is if you don't  know what distributed tracing as you're  probably familiar with logs where most  of the servers in your system have some  logs and the basic idea of distributed  tracing is to take all of the  information about a particular  transaction so it probably is sprinkled  in a bunch of the logs through a bunch  of the servers and tie them all together  so to trace everything that's involved  in serving a particular request so  distributed tracing will build this tree  of what happened and serving the  requests and tie all those things  together both with some metadata about  what happened and also with some timing  information about what happened so let's  look at a trace from that one example  where things took 17 seconds here what  you're looking at and the trace is the  time is progressing from left to right  then this thing in the middle is showing  me the different services that were  traversed as part of this 17.7 second  page  so at the top we started off in  something called crouton I'm and then at  the end we ended up in the service  called live view each one of the rows  here is showing you a single operation  where the width of the bar is the time  taken now the other thing that's really  useful here and helpful for  understanding the Y is understanding the  critical path there's typically a lot of  things that are happening in parallel it  can be tempting to just look for the  things that took the longest but the  only things that are important are the  things that are actually on the critical  path so here the yellow bar is showing  me the critical path and it's showing  you that it's blocked on listing objects  and here what's important is it's helped  me rule something out I think I probably  would have guessed beforehand that the  thing that was slow was actually reading  these objects in that's happening blow  off the screen where it scrolled off but  the important thing is that that's not  on the critical path the only thing on  the critical path is listing the objects  which is the thing that's low so in this  case we optimize the time to list those  objects and then redeployed the code I  mean here you can see time has advanced  a little bit there's that 17.7 second  one in the middle and I can see that  latency has improved a little bit but it  still hasn't improved enough so I need  to do some other things to improve their  performance so the next thing that I  wanted to do was to hone in on this one  part of the histogram where the results  were slow and again this is where the  histogram can be very helpful because  you can hone in on just that one mode of  behavior that you actually care about  now here what I'd ideally like to see is  some idea of correlation can my tool  tell me what aspects of these things  that were slow is correlated with the  fact that they were slow like what's  different about them than the rest of  the operations unfortunately this was  using light stuff which is the tool  we've built which doesn't do this yet I  actually don't know if any tools that do  this I'm so the way that I tend to do  this is by hand so I clicked on a bunch  of the examples from in this histogram  and a bunch from out of them to try and  understand what's different and in this  case what I figured out was distinct  about these requests that were slow is  that they were coming from some  important customer that one of our  customers was reading a lot more data  and that's why the the the page load for  that customer ended up being a lot more  important and what you can see here is  that that thing that I was really  tempted to explain  is just magic or a networking blip  earlier it's true that it wasn't  something that happened hugely often but  it was something that was happening for  one of our biggest and most important  customers and so it actually is very  important to solve and furthermore was a  leading indicator of more problems this  is one of our customers that has the  most data so if we had ignored this  problem it's something that more and  more customers would have experienced  over time as they send more data into  our system so figuring out the source of  this sort of magic issue ends up being  really important so zooming in on the  critical path to understand what's going  on in this slow example here what I can  see now is that the thing that's slow  it's probably hard to see on the left  side here but on what each of these  things are doing is they're  decompressing and unn marshaling the  data so what slow is just deserializing  the data that it's read into some  internal data structure that we can use  so here the question that I would start  asking is contention there's some shared  resource the CPU that in this case the  request is bottlenecking on and what's  competing for the shared resource is  that actually just this one customer's  request that's important or is it there  that is it the case that there are a  bunch of requests happening at the same  time and they're all competing or is  this one request using too much  parallelism there's a talk from Strange  Loop last year from our CTO spoons that  goes into a lot more depth on sort of  visualization tools that you can  understand this contention but again the  sort of trend here is that there's  something slow and I want to hone in on  what exactly is the root cause of it  like what is contending for this shared  resource so to recap the different  things that we did to answer why the  first thing that was important was again  this freshness and horizon like how  quickly can I get the data about what's  going on to analyze it and then how far  back does it go it was useful to get  data that went sort of a day or a week  or a month back so I could compare it to  see if it was normal then all of the  other things on this score color  scorecard you'll notice are things about  reducing the data so one thing that was  helpful was contacts looking at  historical context to understand if it  was normal another thing that's really  useful is looking at value context like  if you see a value for a particular tag  or attribute of the data that looks  weird can you get some context to  understand whether that was differ  than other normal requests a third thing  that was important is the critical path  helping us rule out a bunch of things  that happen in parallel but didn't  actually matter to the response time of  the request we also talked about  correlation trying to separate out which  of the features of the particular things  that are slow are unique to those  operations and doing this sort of just  in time like my system couldn't  necessarily predict in advance which  requests I wanted to correlate to see  that they are different so can it allow  me to do this sort of just-in-time  analysis and then also contention  thinking about what's responsible for  the use of a shared resource and how can  I use that to speed things up a final  why that I didn't talk about much is  error provenance you'll often see a  scenario where there's some error and  it's important to understand which  upstream service it was talking to this  was something that Lita talked about a  little bit in her talk this morning and  that looking at a distributed trace can  be really helpful for doing when you  have some error for example in front-end  user requests and you don't understand  which of the services involved in that  request caused the error  so just to recap this why you'll notice  that pretty much all of these things are  about reducing the data the thing that  was helpful was not getting more data  but understanding which of the data was  actually important so with this context  I want to take a step back and think  about observability as a whole and  what's the scorecard we should think  about when evaluating different  observability tools so this is the  classic sort of scorecard that I think  folks think about when they're thinking  about how to understand their system  they think of these sort of three  checkboxes metrics logs and distributed  tracing and the mentality is often  something like you know Google and  Facebook understand what's going on with  their micro-service architectures and  they use metrics and logs and  distributed tracing so if we use these  three things we can understand our data  too but I think if you talk to folks who  have all of these things many of them  will still tell you that they have no  idea what's going on with their systems  or that they struggle to debug important  problems if you think about each of  these things these things are really  just data and I think the thing that's  more important to understanding the  system is thinking about the actions  that you do with those data with that  data which is really just two things the  first is measuring systems where you  want a lot more data  and the second is answering why to  understand those systems which is about  reducing the search space and we talked  about a sort of checklist earlier in  this talk for each of these things but  at a high level for measuring symptoms  you want more data and that these high  fidelity latency histograms are really  useful for grouping different behaviors  and then for answering why the thing you  want to do is reduce the search space by  having tools that allow you to eliminate  a bunch of potential hypotheses about  things that might have gone wrong so to  recap this talk I think the first and  most important takeaway is that there's  never any magic in your system only the  absence of good tools and if you think  that there's magic in your system it's  probably because your tools aren't good  enough the second is that logging  metrics and tracing aren't enough to  understand your data you need new kinds  of tools to help you understand  performance that will give you good  measurement latency histograms for  example and that will also allow you to  do refinements so you can reduce the  search debates of why building these  tools is hard and I think an interesting  problem if you're interested working on  that come talk to me more and with that  I'm happy to take any questions and also  feel free to email me if you have  questions offline  [Applause]
 

